UPDATE sections SET intro_tex = 'Fix a \hyperref[8JH4]{primitive character} $\psi$ of \hyperref[8JA9]{order} $o$. This chapter sketches why the \hyperref[8JG8]{weighted sum} $\mathcal G_\psi$ satisfies the \hyperref[8JK2]{interval estimate}. The proof does not bound $\mathcal G_\psi$ directly. It bounds a weighted count of prime powers, sorted by the classes of the primes in a finite abelian group (\ref{8JU4}). Fourier analysis on that group writes the count as a combination of \hyperref[8JF7]{smoothed prime sums} $G_\xi$, one for each character $\xi$, and suitable averages then keep the members of the \hyperref[8JG8]{cyclic family} with the right weights, plus a few small extra terms (\ref{8JU5}). This section explains the averaging and fixes the data it needs: a group $A$, a subgroup $A_0$, a set $T \subseteq A$, and two auxiliary primes $q$ and $d$.

\textbf{The group $A$.} For a suitable modulus $M$, the group $A$ is a quotient of $(\Z/M\Z)^\times$, written additively. So every prime $p \nmid M$ has a class in $A$, the image of $p \bmod M$, and the characters of $A$, that is the homomorphisms $\xi\colon A \to \C^\times$, are \hyperref[8JA9]{Dirichlet characters} modulo $M$. In the language of Galois theory, $A$ is part of the Galois group of an abelian field $E \subseteq \Q(\zeta_M)$, and the class of $p$ is its Frobenius element. The field $E$ is built from the field cut out by $\psi$, the real cyclotomic field $\Q(\zeta_{2^k} + \zeta_{2^k}^{-1})$ and the quadratic fields $\Q(\sqrt{-q})$ and $\Q(\sqrt{-d})$ of the auxiliary primes below \cite[Section 3]{Outline}. Here $k$ is chosen with $x^{2+8\varepsilon} \le 2^{k-2} \le 2x^{2+8\varepsilon}$, so $A$ is large, and $A$, $A_0$ and $T$ depend on $x$.

\textbf{Averaging over $A_0$.} For a subgroup $A_0 \subseteq A$ and a character $\xi$ of $A$,
\[ \frac{1}{|A_0|} \sum_{u \in A_0} \xi(u) = \begin{cases} 1, & \xi(u) = 1 \text{ for all } u \in A_0, \\ 0, & \text{otherwise}, \end{cases} \]
because if $\xi(v) \ne 1$ for some $v \in A_0$, replacing $u$ by $u + v$ multiplies the sum by $\xi(v)$. So averaging over $A_0$ removes every character that is not trivial on $A_0$. We take $A_0$ of index $o$ such that the characters trivial on $A_0$ are exactly the powers $\psi^0, \psi^1, \dots, \psi^{o-1}$, read modulo $M$. The primitive characters inducing them form the cyclic family $\mathcal C_\psi$.

\textbf{Averaging over $T$.} A second average, over a set $T \subseteq A$ of \emph{translates} (each $t \in T$ gives a shifted copy of the count in \ref{8JU4}), produces the weights. Suppose that the fraction of $T$ lying in the coset $\alpha + A_0$ is
\[ \lambda_\alpha = \begin{cases} \dfrac{4}{3o+1}, & \alpha \in A_0, \\[6pt] \dfrac{3}{3o+1}, & \alpha \notin A_0. \end{cases} \]
These fractions add up to $1$, since there are $o - 1$ cosets other than $A_0$. A character $\xi$ trivial on $A_0$ is constant on each coset, and $\sum_{\alpha \in A/A_0} \xi(\alpha)$ is $o$ for the trivial character and $0$ otherwise, by the same argument as above. Hence
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{|T|} \sum_{t \in T} \xi(t) &= \sum_{\alpha \in A/A_0} \lambda_\alpha\, \xi(\alpha) \\
&= \frac{3}{3o+1} \sum_{\alpha \in A/A_0} \xi(\alpha) + \frac{1}{3o+1} \\
&= \begin{cases} 1, & \xi \text{ trivial}, \\[2pt] \dfrac{1}{3o+1}, & \xi \text{ nontrivial}. \end{cases}
\end{align*}
These are the weights $a_{\mathbf 1} = 1$ and $a_\chi = 1/(3o+1)$ of $\mathcal G_\psi$. \ref{8JU7} achieves the fractions $\lambda_\alpha$ up to an error $x^{-1+\varepsilon}$.

\textbf{The primes $q$ and $d$.} The count in \ref{8JU4} actually uses the slightly larger group $B = (\Z/2\Z)^2 \times A$, which is also a quotient of $(\Z/M\Z)^\times$. Averaging over $A_0$ therefore keeps $4o$ characters of $B$, not only the $o$ powers of $\psi$. Each extra character is a Dirichlet character modulo the conductor of $\psi$ times $\bigl(\frac{a}{q}\bigr)$, $\bigl(\frac{a}{d}\bigr)$ or both, the Legendre symbols modulo the two auxiliary primes $q$ and $d$ \cite[Section 11]{Outline}. These primes are chosen so that the smoothed prime sums of the extra characters are at most $x^{1/2+\varepsilon}$ in absolute value. They then contribute only the error term $\Sigma(x)$ of \ref{8JUD}. Such primes exist because bad ones are rare: a mean-square estimate shows that at least half of the candidate primes $q$ (in a prescribed residue class and size range) work, and then the same holds for $d$.

\textbf{One interval for everything.} The \hyperref[8JP5]{interval criterion} needs the bound at every $x$ in one interval $[Y, Y^{3/2}]$. So $q$ and $d$ are fixed once for the whole interval, while $A$, $A_0$ and $T$ are chosen anew at each $x$ in it. At the same $x$ two deep inputs must hold as well: the \hyperref[8JU8]{cohomological length bound} and the theta-lift data used in \ref{8JU4}. They are available only on certain ranges of $x$, not at every large $x$. \ref{8JU7} finds arbitrarily distant intervals $[Y, Y^{3/2}]$ on which all these conditions hold together. This is why the interval estimate is claimed only on a sparse sequence of intervals. The deep inputs come from the Lean development; on a first reading, treat them as black boxes.', intro_html_cache = NULL, revision = revision + 1, updated_at = '2026-11-24 23:05:00', updated_by = 'claude' WHERE id = 106;
